##* {{quote-book|en|author=David Egan|chapter=Wittgenstein’s Pursuit of Authenticity in Philosophy|title=The Pursuit of an Authentic Philosophy: [[w:Ludwig Wittgenstein|Wittgenstein]], [[w:Martin Heidegger|Heidegger]], and the Everyday|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2019|section=part III (The Pursuit of an Authentic Philosophy)|page=222|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yO-MDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA222|isbn=978-0-19-883263-8|passage=[P]hilosophers often press thought experiments into service as a way of addressing a given philosophical problem by analogy: {{...}} Thomson thinks the question of whether we are permitted to unplug ourselves from an unconscious violinist to whom we have been unwittingly attached admits of a fairly clear answer, and wants to bring that clarity to the '''muddier''' issue of abortion.}}
