###* {{RQ:Bunyan Works|volume=III|title=Water of Life|page=216|column=2|passage=If their doctrines are '''muddy''', their notions are '''muddy''': if their doctrines are bloody, their notions and tempers are bloody: but if their doctrines are clear, so are their notions, for their doctrine has given them a clear understanding of things.}}
###* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|page=115|passage=On the ſcheme of this barbarous philoſophy, vvhich is the offſpring of cold hearts and '''muddy''' underſtandings, {{...}} lavvs are to be ſupported only by their ovvn terrors, and by the concern, vvhich each individual may find in them, from his ovvn private ſpeculations, or can ſpare to them from his ovvn private intereſts.}}
###* {{RQ:Wollstonecraft Vindication Women|chapter=The Prevailing Opinion of a Sexual Character Discussed|page=46|passage=[S]oldiers acquire a little ſuperficial knovvledge, ſnatched from the '''muddy''' current of converſation, and, from continually mixing vvith ſociety, they gain, vvhat is termed a knovvledge of the vvorld; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Thackeray Paris Sketch Book|volume=II|chapter=Madame [[w:George Sand|[George] Sand]] and the New Apocalypse|page=105|passage=Wretched as {{w|Voltaire}}'s sneers and puns are, I think there is something more manly and earnest even in them, than in the present '''muddy''' French transcendentalism.}}
