### To [[confuse]] (a [[person#Noun|person]] or their [[thinking#Noun|thinking]]); to [[muddle#Verb|muddle]].
###: {{ux|en|The discussion only '''muddied''' their understanding of the subject.}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet|act=IV|scene=v|page=273|column=2|passage=VVhen ſorrovves comes,{{sic|come}} they come not ſingle ſpies, / But in Battalia[n]es. Firſt, her Father ſlaine, / Next your Sonne gone, and he moſt violent Author / Of his ovvne iuſt remoue: the people '''muddied''', / Thicke and vnvvholſome in their thoughts, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Wollstonecraft Vindication Women|chapter=Of the Pernicious Effects which Arise from the Unnatural Distinctions Established in Society|page=255|passage=I only recreated an imagination, fatigued by contemplating the vices and follies vvhich all proceed from a feculent ſtream of vvealth that has '''muddied''' the pure rills of natural affection, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Scott Rob Roy|volume=I|page=278|passage=Habitual topers, I believe, acquire the power of soaking themselves with a quantity of liquor which does little more than '''muddy''' those intellects, that, in their sober state, are none of the clearest; {{...}}}}
